{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]],"date-time":"2026-09-29T07:03:12Z","timestamp":1790665392344,"version":"4.1.0"},"reference-count":92,"publisher":"American Astronomical Society","issue":"2","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]],"date-time":"2026-09-29T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1790640000000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"},{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]],"date-time":"2026-09-29T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1790640000000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/iopscience.iop.org\/info\/page\/text-and-data-mining"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["iopscience.iop.org"],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["ApJ"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    Here, we present experiments exploring the effect of initial ice composition on the chemistry of mixed H\n                    <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n                    O+CO+NH\n                    <jats:sub>3<\/jats:sub>\n                    interstellar ice analogs subjected to UV irradiation and temperature-programmed desorption (TPD). The ices were monitored with Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy, while the desorbed gas-phase species were monitored with a quadrupole mass spectrometer (QMS). These experiments show that ammonia (NH\n                    <jats:sub>3<\/jats:sub>\n                    ) UV destruction is facilitated by dilution in binary and ternary mixed ices, while carbon monoxide (CO) destruction is dominated by interactions with water (H\n                    <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n                    O) dissociation products, leading primarily to formation of CO\n                    <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n                    . A number of other products are formed, with the ammonium ion (NH\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      <\/jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:msubsup>\n                          <mml:mrow\/>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mn>4<\/mml:mn>\n                          <\/mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mo>+<\/mml:mo>\n                          <\/mml:mrow>\n                        <\/mml:msubsup>\n                      <\/mml:math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    ), formaldehyde (H\n                    <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n                    CO), the cyanate ion (OCN\n                    <jats:sup>\u2212<\/jats:sup>\n                    ), and formamide (NH\n                    <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n                    CHO) being the most abundant products after CO\n                    <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n                    . The formation of NH\n                    <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n                    CHO is heavily dependent on ice composition and shows different behavior when CO or NH\n                    <jats:sub>3<\/jats:sub>\n                    is in excess. Desorption temperatures of NH\n                    <jats:sub>3<\/jats:sub>\n                    and CO are higher with higher percentages of H\n                    <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n                    O. Ices with higher percentages of CO tend to have stronger IR features arising from the dangling OH stretch and demonstrate enhanced formation and reduced trapping of carbon dioxide (CO\n                    <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n                    ). Even modest alterations in the initial ice composition have a large effect on the destruction of precursors and the formation of photoproducts. These results highlight the importance of experimental studies of mixed ices, particularly H\n                    <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n                    O-dominated ices.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3847\/1538-4357\/ae99c4","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]],"date-time":"2026-09-29T06:25:28Z","timestamp":1790663128000},"page":"218","update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1088\/crossmark-policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Irradiation and Sublimation of Astrophysically Relevant H\n                    <sub>2<\/sub>\n                    O:NH\n                    <sub>3<\/sub>\n                    :CO Ices"],"prefix":"10.3847","volume":"1009","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-2333-7204","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ruby W.","family":"Neisser","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Wisconsin-Madison, 1101 University Ave."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4042-3609","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Catherine E.","family":"Walker","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Wisconsin-Madison, 1101 University Ave."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2270-6103","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Gustavo A.","family":"Cruz-Diaz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Wisconsin-Madison, 475 N Charter St."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3976-322X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Collette C.","family":"Sarver","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Wisconsin-Madison, 1101 University Ave."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-8404-460X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Colton R.","family":"Moore","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Wisconsin-Madison, 1101 